## a b s t r a c t
The capacity attenuation of transition metal oxides (TMOs) and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) is the obstacle for practical application in lithium ion batteries, due to the extensive volume variation upon charge/discharge cycles. Herein, a hierarchical composite material with copper oxide $(\mathsf{C u O})$ multi-yolks and copper-1, 3, 5-benzenetricarboxylate (Cu-BTC) shell is synthesized by a facile method to study the effect of the hierarchical structure on the electrochemical performance. The porosity and pore volume of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC composites are optimized to buffer the volume change and facilitate the infiltration of electrolytes by altering reaction conditions. The ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ with the largest surface area and pore volume delivers an excellent reversible capacity of $780.7\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $200\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles, and ultrastable long-term performance with a specific capacity of $569\ \mathrm{mAh\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 900 cycles. The corresponding full battery shows moderate capacity retention from 149.4 to $125.8\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 70 cycles, with a specific capacity retention of $84.2\%$ , based on the mass of lithium iron phosphate $\left(\mathrm{LiFePO}_{4}\right).$ ) at $0.2\mathrm{~C~}$ ( $1{\dot{\mathrm{~C}}}=170{\mathrm{~mA~g}}^{-1}.$ . This strategy applies copper oxide as the metal source of the coordination compound, as well as the internal yolks, which can be extended to the in-situ construction of other hierarchical composites, providing a new avenue for practical application of TMOs and MOFs as anode materials.
