## 1. Introduction
Searching for desired electrode materials with high energy density and long life cycle performance is vital for the future development of lithium ion batteries (LIBs).[1] Many efforts have been carried out to upgrade the capacity of the anode materials, and plentiful progress has been made.[2–5] Compared with conventional graphite anodes, transition metal oxides (TMOs) have been viewed as promising anode materials for next-generation LIBs because of their high theoretical specific capacities, high abundance, economic affordability, and environmental compatibility. [6–10] Meanwhile, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have also been identified as potential functional materials for LIBs and display high specific capacities, because of their adjustable pore structure, high chemical stability, and sufficient metal ions and ligands. [11,12] For example, M BTC materials $\mathrm{\DeltaM=Cu}$ , Co, Mn, etc.) have displayed excellent lithium-storage performances,[13–15] attributed to the multiple electronic redox conversion of both metal ions and BTC ligands.[16]
The rich redox chemistry endows TMOs and MOFs with excellent lithium storage, and therefore high capacities are within basic expectations nowadays. However, both materials still face many challenges in practical applications. Firstly, capacity attenuation remains a major obstacle for commercialization, caused by the extensive volume variation during insertion/extraction processes. Secondly, undesirable rate performance dragged by sluggish kinetics at higher current densities results in low charge/discharge efficiency, which is infeasible for quick charge replenishment. To address these issues, a simple strategy is developed to construct a material with nanosized and porous structure,[17–20] which possesses shorter ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ion diffusion length, prohibits the structure decomposition during repeated cycles, and exposes more superficial reaction sites. Besides, controllable composites often demonstrate improved structural stability, also favorable to deliver a higher capacity than a single component, and improve the cycling stability of electrode materials.[21–24] Benefiting from these advantages, better rate performance, enhanced cycle stability, and higher specific capacity can be expected.
Hierarchical composites can integrate the merits of nanosized structure, pores (macropore/mesopore/micropore), and the synergy interaction of diverse components.[25–29] The pores can facilitate the infiltration of electrolytes, and buffer volume change of the internal nanosized particles, resulting in favorable materials for LIBs. However, the synthesis and the consequent separation of hierarchical composites are generally tedious, energy-consuming, and intricate. Especially, CuO and Cu-based MOFs are both typical materials with high theoretical capacity and unstable cycle performance.[30–33] Therefore, developing a novel facile method to build delicate hierarchical structures to improve the electrochemical performance of $\mathtt{C u O}$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC anodes is highly desired.
Herein, we introduce a simple way to obtain hierarchical ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC with a multi-yolk-shell octahedral morphology by using CuO as a template and the source of copper ions. The synthesis involves a simple process to obtain the CuO precursor, followed by stirring with $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ ligand in mixed water and ethanol. The hierarchical ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC can be constructed by in-situ conversion and the following self-assembly. Combined with the advantages of unique structure, easy operation, and eco-friendliness, this strategy provides new guidelines for the structure optimization of the functional material, which opens up new avenues to promote the application of TMO@MOFs in energy storage fields.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
$\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ (AR, $99\%$ ), ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ (AR, $299.5\%$ , $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ (AR, $99\%$ ), $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ $(98.0\%)$ were purchased from Macklin (Shanghai, China). Carboxymethyl cellulose sodium (CMC 2200, $299\%$ ), polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF HSV900), and LiFe $\mathsf{P O}_{4}$ $(\geq98\%)$ were purchased from DoDoChem, and all chemicals were used directly without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of the CuO precursor
The $\mathtt{C u O}$ precursor was prepared by the direct thermal decomposition method.[34] In a typical synthesis, $12.5~\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $5.3\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ were dissolved in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of deionized water, separately (Solution A and B). Then, Solution B was added to Solution A and sonicated at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The green precipitate was then collected by centrifugation, washed with warm deionized water several times, and dried at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $6\mathrm{h}$ . Finally, it was decomposed in a pre-heated muffle furnace at $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.3. Synthesis of hierarchical multi-yolk-shell CuO@Cu-BTC
$50~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathtt{C u O}$ precursor was dispersed in a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ solution mixture (EtOH: $\mathrm{H}_{2}0=1{:}1\ \mathrm{v}01\%$ by ultrasonication, and $15.8\mathrm{~g~H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ was dissolved in a $200~\mathrm{{mL}}$ solution mixture (EtOH: $\mathrm{H}_{2}0=1{:}1$ vol $\%$ ). After that, the CuO dispersion was added slowly into the $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ solution under stirring for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ The solution mixture was then oil-bathed at $40~^{\circ}C$ with constant stirring for 5, 10, 15, 20, and $25\mathrm{~h~}$ , respectively. The precipitate was collected by centrifugation, washed with anhydrous ethanol several times, and dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . As a control sample, the pristine Cu-BTC was synthesized according to the reported method.[35]
## 2.4. Material characterization
The morphology was characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi S-4800) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2100F). The structure and composition of samples were studied by X-ray diffraction (XRD-6000, Shimadzu) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific ESCA-LAB250-Xi. The FT-IR spectra were recorded by an infrared spectrophotometer (IS10, ThermoFisher). Thermogravimetric analysis (TG) was carried out in the air, using a Pyris Diamond equipment (Perkin Elmer). The surface area and pore analysis were carried out on a BelsorpMax apparatus with $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ as absorbate at $77~\mathrm{K}$
## 2.5. Electrochemical measurements
The coin cells (CR2032) were assembled in a glove box (OMNILAB, VAC) filled with argon. Lithium metal was used as the counter/reference electrode, the Celgard 2500 was applied as a separator, and the electrolyte was composed of $1.0\mathrm{~M~LiPF_{6}}$ in ethylene carbonate (EC) and diethyl carbonate (DEC) $(1{:}1\ \mathrm{vol}\%)$ with $5\%$ fluorinated ethylene carbonate (FEC). All sample powders were activated in a vacuum oven at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then active materials $(70\mathrm{\ur\%})$ , Super P $(20\mathrm{\wt\%})$ , and CMC $(10~\mathrm{wt\%})$ were mixed in deionized water and cast onto a copper foil, dried in a vacuum at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . On each electrode, the loading mass of active material was in the range of $1.5{-}2.0~\mathrm{mg}$ . The charge–discharge performance of the battery was recorded on the LAND CT2001A multichannel battery test system. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were recorded on a CHI760D electrochemical workstation at a scanning rate of $0.2~\mathrm{\mV}~s^{-1}$ during $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{~V~}$ Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurement was carried out by Autolab PGSTAT302N electrochemical workstation in a frequency range of $10^{5}$ to $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ and the AC amplitude was $5~\mathrm{mV}$ .
For the tests of full batteries, commercially available ${\mathrm{LiFePO}}_{4}$ was used as the cathode. LiFeP $\mathrm{\Delta})_{\mathrm{{O_{4}}}}$ $(80~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , Super P $10\mathrm{\wt\%})$ , and PVDF (10 wt%) were mixed in $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) and cast onto an Al foil, dried in vacuum at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . Before assembling the full batteries, the ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC electrodes were treated by the pre-lithiation process to decrease the high initial irreversible capacity.[36] The loading mass of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC anodes and $\mathsf{L i F e P O}_{4}$ cathodes were $0.7{-}0.9{\mathrm{~mg}}$ and $3.0{-}4.0~\mathrm{mg}$ , respectively. The as-prepared full batteries were cycled between 0.3 and $4.0\mathrm{V}.$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
The XRD spectra proved the conversion from $\mathtt{C u O}$ to ${\mathsf{C u O@C u-}}$ BTC (Fig. 1a). The characteristic peaks of the CuO precursor can be well assigned to the monoclinic phase of CuO (JCPDS No. 48– 1548). After reaction in $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ , no new prominent peaks appeared, which indicated the initial conversion was relatively slow. After $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ , new diffraction peaks at 6.71, 9.49, 11.62, 13.44, and $25.96^{\circ}$ emerged, which can be indexed to the (200), (220), (222), (400), and (731) planes of as-obtained Cu-BTC, verifying the formation of $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ .[37] The peaks corresponding to Cu-BTC increased with the prolonged reaction time while the peaks of $\mathtt{C u O}$ decreased continuously, in agreement with the growth of $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC and the reduction of $\mathtt{C u O}$ . After $25\mathrm{h}$ a tiny peak located at $8.2^{\circ}$ appeared, which is the characteristic peak of [Cu $(\mathrm{BTC-H}_{2})_{2}\bullet(\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0})_{2}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ (Fig. S1, Supporting Information), declaring the generation of trace amounts of the phase.[38] The FT-IR spectra of the CuO precursor, pristine Cu-BTC, and CuO@Cu-BTC composites are shown in Fig. 1b. The bands at 1627&1558 (asymmetric tensile vibration of $-\mathsf{C O O^{-}})$ , 1444&1371 (symmetrical tensile vibration of $-\mathsf{C O O^{-}})$ , and $111387618721\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (out-of-plane vibrations of ligands) corresponded to the characteristic bands of
Cu-BTC, respectively.[13,39] The absorption band around $504\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was the specific vibration of the Cu-O bonds,[40] and it gradually weakened with the increasing reaction time, indicating that the content of CuO was decreasing, which was consistent with the XRD spectrum. The XRD and FT-IR spectrum indicate both the $\mathtt{C u O}$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC were present in the composite products.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted to analyze CuO, pristine Cu-BTC, and CuO@Cu-BTC $(20\mathrm{h})$ composite. The profile in Fig. S2 proves the existence of Cu, O, and C elements in the sample. In the high-resolution $\mathtt{C u2p}$ spectrum (Fig. 1c), the Cu $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peak at $933.6\ \mathrm{~eV}$ and Cu $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ peak at $953.5\ \mathrm{~eV}$ in ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\mathrm{~h})$ can be assigned to CuO,[41] while the Cu $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peak at 934.6 eV and the Cu $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ peak at $954.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be indexed to Cu-BTC.[16] In Fig. 1d, the O 1s peak of CuO precursor was observed at $529.9\mathrm{eV}$ , corresponds to the binding energy value of $\mathtt{C u-O}$ , and the other peak at $531.7\mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to O-AO bond, caused by the physically adsorbed $0_{2}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ .[42,43] In the O 1s signal of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\mathrm{h})$ , the presence of residual CuO was manifested by the peak at 530.1 eV. The other two peaks of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ at 531.7 and $533.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to the Cu-BTC, consistent with the signals of the pristine control sample. These features coincided with the results above.
The porous characteristic of $\mathsf{C u O@C u}$ -BTC was determined by nitrogen adsorption/desorption analysis (Fig. 1e&S3). The Brunner-Emmet-Teller (BET) values of CuO precursor and ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC (5, 10, 15, 20, and $25\mathrm{~h~}$ ) were 7.73, 150.13, 617.40, 931.39, 1476.80, and $1020.40~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, with pore volumes of 0.044, 0.106, 0.27, 0.40, 0.62, and $0.42{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively. Basically, the BET surface areas are due to Cu-BTC, which is well known as microporous material,[44] while $\mathtt{C u O}$ is nonporous. Hence, the BET surface area increased to a maximum with enriched Cu-BTC contents in prolonged reaction time from 0 to $20\mathrm{~h~}$ , as $\mathtt{C u O}$ conversed to Cu-BTC gradually. However, when the reaction time is prolonged further to $25\mathrm{~h~}$ , the BET surface area decreased due to the formation of the less porous $[\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{B T C-H}_{2})_{2},$ ∙ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ instead of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BTC})}_{2}(\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O})}_{3}]_{\mathrm{{n}}}$ . The content ratios of CuO in the composites were quantified by thermogravimetric analysis (Fig. 1f), which were $92.13\%$ , $46.52\%$ , $28.65\%$ , $17.82\%$ , and $8.53\%$ , respectively for ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC (5, 10, 15, 20, and $25\mathrm{~h~}$ ), confirming the gradual decrease of $\mathtt{C u O}$ and the enrichment of Cu-BTC, and the slowing down conversion rate (the calculation details are listed in supporting information).
The in-situ conversion from CuO to Cu-BTC was observed with FESEM and TEM (Fig. 2&S4), and the self-assembly into the octahedral structure is described in Scheme 1. The original $\mathtt{C u O}$ precursor displayed a morphology of aggregated particles with an average size of about $70~\mathrm{nm}$ and a smooth surface (Fig. S4a, b, c). After reaction in the $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ solution for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ , the size of the particles scarcely changed, indicating sluggish reaction kinetics in the stage. The surface of the $\mathtt{C u O}$ precursor became rough, and in the inset TEM image, the dark precursor cores were wrapped by a brighter shell, showing the initial core–shell structure (Fig. S4d, e, f). After $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ , an octahedral rudiment with an irregular profile (Fig. S4g, h) was initially formed, which is the typical morphology of Cu-BTC. At the same time, multiple uneven cores corresponding to the residual precursor were scattered in the octahedrons, so the composite demonstrated a hierarchical multi-yolk-shell morphology (Fig. S4i). After $15\mathrm{h}$ , the octahedral structure was further matured, and multiple inner yolks showed clear margins and a smaller size (Fig. S4j, k, l).
With the reaction time prolonged to $20\mathrm{~h~}$ , the ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\mathrm{h})$ composite displayed angular octahedrons with hierarchical structure (Fig. 2a, b, c), and the TEM images confirm that the smaller yolks disappeared. Meanwhile the size distribution of internal cores was narrowed, and the multiple CuO yolks decreased to an average diameter of $7.32\ \mathrm{nm}$ . HRTEM images of CuO@Cu-BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ (Fig. 2d, e) reveal the typical lattice fringe spacing of dark yolks was $0.252\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (11–1) plane of the monoclinic $\mathtt{C u O}$ crystal. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Fig. 2f) confirms the monoclinic phase of the CuO yolks with the polycrystalline diffraction rings, corresponding to the (111), (200), (220), and (311) planes, respectively. Meanwhile, the element mapping images (Fig. 2g-j) confirm the uniform distribution of $\mathtt{C u}$ (in yellow) and O (in orange) in the octahedrons. After reaction with $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ for $25\mathrm{~h~}$ , the octahedral morphology began to decompose, and rod-like crystals formed on the surface of octahedrons (Fig. $\mathbf{S4m,n,0})$ , agree to the phase change from $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}(-$ $\mathsf{H}_{2}0)_{3}\bigr]_{\mathrm{n}}$ to $\mathrm{|Cu(BTC–H_{2})_{2}\bullet(H_{2}O)_{2}|\bullet3H_{2}O}$ . The pristine Cu-BTC prepared by the solvothermal method showed an octahedral shape with a size between 2 and $3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. $\mathbf{\l{S4p,q,r})}$ . The hierarchical structure of $\mathsf{C u O@C u}$ -BTC composites regulated the porosity and pore volume, which can lead to different electrochemical performances. Compared to ordinary conversion anode materials, the hierarchical characteristics of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC can facilitate the diffusion of electrolyte and buffer the volume variation of $\mathtt{C u O}$ and CuBTC, thus giving rise to a high specific capacity and excellent cycling stability of the electrodes.
According to the above results, the construction process of the ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC with multi-yolk-shell structure can be depicted in Scheme 1. First, the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions dissociated from the CuO precursor slowly and coordinated with the $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ ligand. The resultant CuBTC was wrapped on the surface of the $\mathtt{C u O}$ precursor to form the initial core–shell $\mathsf{C u O@C u}$ -BTC nanoparticles. Next, the CuO precursor continued releasing ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ to coordinate with more $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ ligand. Meanwhile, the initial sphere-like CuO@Cu-BTC nanoparticles aggregated and self-assembled into octahedrons. Then the CuO precursor kept on releasing ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , so the size of CuO yolks decreased, and the octahedral morphology got improved.
The CV curves of the $\mathtt{C u O}$ precursors, ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC composites, and pristine Cu-BTC for the first four cycles at a scan rate of $0.2\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ within the voltage range of $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{~V~}$ are given in Fig. 3a&S5. The cathodic peaks of CuO precursor were located at 1.29 and $0.79\mathrm{~V~}$ , corresponding to the two-step reduction from ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{C u}^{0},[26,45-47]$ and the anodic peak was located at $2.52~\mathrm{V}.$ . The cathodic and anodic peaks of pristine $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC were broad and flat, located at 0.50 and $2.61\mathrm{\V}$ , respectively, suggesting the insertion/extraction of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions to the BTC ligand and the redox reaction.[13–15,48] The integral areas of the redox peaks in the hierarchical ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC composites, which were contributed by both $\mathtt{C u O}$ and Cu-BTC components, altered with the content percentage and yolk size of CuO. ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ exhibited two pronounced peaks at 1.15 and $0.11~\mathrm{V}$ in the first cathodic scan (Fig. 3a), which can be ascribed to the formation of the solid electrolyte interface (SEI) film. In the initial anodic scan, a broad peak around $0.80\mathrm{V}$ was corresponded to the decomposition of SEI film, [16] and the prominent peak at $2.59~\mathrm{V}$ signified the oxidation of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and the extraction of lithium from the ligand.[17] During the subsequent scans, cathodic peaks were observed at $1.29{\mathrm{V}}$ $0.79{\mathrm{~V~}}$ , and $_{0.41\mathrm{~V~}}$ , in accordance with the lithiation processes of CuO and $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC, meanwhile the anodic peak at $2.59{\mathrm{~V~}}$ remained. The latter CV curves of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC in the 2–4 cycles overlapped, indicating highly reversible electrochemical reactions and excellent structural stability. Fig. 3b&S6 show the voltage-specific capacity curves of all anode materials for the 1st, 50th, and 100th cycles at a current density of $200\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In the first galvanostatic process of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ , plateaus appeared at 1.2 and $0.2~\mathrm{V}$ upon discharge, and $0.8{\mathrm{~V~}}$ upon the charge, corresponding to the formation and decomposition of SEI membrane, respectively. In the following discharge/charge process, the lithiation and delithiation plateaus were close to the cathodic and anodic peak positions of CV curves, corresponding to the multi-step electrochemical reactions of CuO and Cu-BTC. The profiles of other $\mathsf{C u O@C u}$ -BTC (5, 10, 15, and $25\mathrm{~h~}$ ) composites were almost duplicates of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC (20 h), indicating similar reactions.
The cycling performances of all samples were studied at $200\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 3c&S7a, the charge/discharge specific capacities were 333.1/871.1, 412.5/1311.7, 466.1/1022.7, 588.5/1156, 684.7/1286.7, 792.8/1365.8, and 631.9/1479 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for CuO precursor, pristine Cu-BTC and $\mathsf{C u O@C u}$ -BTC (5, 10, 15, 20, and $25{\mathrm{~h}}^{\prime}$ , respectively, with initial coulombic efficiencies (ICE) of $38.24\%$ , $31.45\%$ , $45.57\%$ , $50.91\%$ , $53.24\%$ , $58.04\%$ and $42.73\%$ , respectively. The results show that the discharge capacity and ICE increased with the growing reaction time from $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ to $20\mathrm{~h~}$ , but turned down in the ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC (25 h). This trend was consistent with the alteration of surface areas, as the hierarchical structure regulated the porosity. The greater surface area can expose more active sites to accommodate lithium ions, and the larger pore volume can facilitate the infiltration of electrolytes, leading to the optimal capacity and ICE of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ . Moreover, the hierarchical multi-yolk-shell structure also improves the cycle stability. As depicted in Fig. 3c&S7a, the specific capacities of pristine $\mathtt{C u O}$ and Cu-BTC degraded gradually to 300.7 and $311.2\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in 100 cycles, at a current density of $200\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ , while all the ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC composites showed superior cycle stability. The ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC (5, 10, 15, 20, and $25\mathrm{~h~}$ ) composites retained 551.3, 630.3, 672, 780.7, and 630.4 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, after 100 cycles. The basic electrochemical properties of the ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ electrode were listed and compared to the other reported CuO-based, Cu-BTC-based, and the relative anode materials, as Table S1 shows. The reported specific capacity of $\mathtt{C u O}$ -based materials can barely reach the theoretical value of $670\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ,[49] and the record high stable performance of Cu-BTC was merely $626.4\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ,[50] whereas the ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ composite delivered a steady performance better than any pristine or composite counterparts. The capacity contribution of Cu-BTC with weight percentages of $7.87\%$ , $53.48\%$ , $71.35\%$ , $82.18\%$ , and $91.47\%$ in the CuO@CuBTC (5, 10, 15, 20 and $25\mathrm{~h~}$ ) were calculated to be $31.10\%$ , $69.57\%$ , $82.42\%$ , $90.59\%$ , and $94.42\%$ respectively (Table S2).
The poor cycling stability of $\mathtt{C u O}$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC were generally ascribed to the volume variation and collapse upon discharge/ charge cycles,[13,49–51] while the hierarchical structure of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC buffered the fluctuation of particle size, exhibited steady performances throughout 100 cycles. Meanwhile, the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ on the surface of CuO yolks can coordinate with carboxylic acid ligands in the shell to form inorganic–organic-inorganic heterogeneous composites and stabilize the yolk-shell structure,[40,52] also leading to excellent stability. Besides, the residual CuO encapsulated in Cu-BTC was downsized based on the dissociation of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , which alleviated the internal stress of volume change.
The rate performances were investigated and shown in Fig. 3d. At the current densities of 100, 200, 500, 1000, and $2000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $\mathsf{C u O@C u}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ delivered specific capacities of 845.1, 805.9, 686.2, 586.9, and $489.3\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, and then recovered to 832.2 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when the current density went back to $100\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ . As a contrast, the CuO precursor (371.6, 337.1, 252.6, 149.3, and $58.8\ \mathrm{\mAh}\ \mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ ) and pristine Cu-BTC (397.4, 331.5, 237.3, 163.9, and $96.6\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ) exhibited inferior performance at any current density. The performances of other samples were shown in Fig. S7b, and ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC (20 h) outperformed the other hierarchical composites, in accord with the galvanostatic cycling test.
The cycle stability of electrodes is essential for practical application, especially at high current densities. The long-term cycle stability of the CuO precursor, pristine $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC, and CuO@Cu-BTC composites was investigated furtherly at higher current densities (Fig. 3e&S7c, d). The $\mathsf{C u O@C u}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ composite delivered an initial specific discharge/charge capacity of $1156.5/556.5\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ retained a reversible capacity of $569\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 900 cycles at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , while the pristine $\mathtt{C u O}$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC degraded to 113.7 and $54.5\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, in 250 cycles due to the collapse of the crystal structure. All the hierarchical ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC (5, 10, 15, 20, and $25{\mathrm{~h}}^{\prime}{\mathrm{~}}$ ) composites showed improved cycle stability at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , exhibiting better stability than CuO and $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC. At higher current densities of 2 and $4\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , the reversible capacities of the ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{~h~})$ after 1000 cycles were 342.7 and $152.1\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The hierarchical multi-yolk-shell buffered the volume variation, and the regulation of yolk size and porosity led to the optimization of electrochemical property, resulting in the ultrastable performance of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\mathrm{~h})$ . The CuO@Cu-BTC (20 h) electrode at a current of $1000\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ after 500 cycles was disassembled from the cell and investigated by TEM (Fig. S8). Small fragments were observed while the octahedral profile remained. Therefore, the decomposition under the high current density after an extended cycle test was quite limited, giving rise to the ultrastable performance. The hierarchical multi-yolk-shell composite exhibited an outstanding capacity and an ultrastable cycle performance.
The high capacity and stability of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC composites can also be determined by the EIS (Fig. S9). The semicircles in the highfrequency region were deemed to be the charge transfer resistance of the CuO precursor, CuO@Cu-BTC (5, 10, 15, 20, and $25{\mathrm{~h~}}$ ) and pristine $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC electrodes, and the diameters changed from 183.2, 196.3, 119.2, 107.4, 97.6, 163.4, and $187.1\ \Omega$ before cycle to 130.2, 160.1, 87.5, 55.3, 38.6, 64.2, and $105.7\Omega$ after cycle tests, respectively. The lithium-ion diffusion coefficients $(\mathrm{D_{Li}^{+}})$ of the electrodes were calculated from the low-frequency region of the EIS spectra based on the following equation $\mathrm{\bar{D}}=\mathrm{\bar{R}}^{2}\mathrm{T}^{2}/(2\mathrm{A}^{2}\mathrm{n}^{4}\mathrm{F}^{4}\mathrm{C}^{2}\mathrm{\upsigma}^{2}),$ [53] where A, n, F, C, R, and T represent the electrode area, reactive electron number per chemical formula, Faraday0s constant, the number of electrons per molecule attending electronic transfer reaction, gas constant, and the testing temperature, respectively. The Warburg coefficient $(\upsigma)$ is the slope of the plots of $Z^{\prime}$ versus $\mathbf{\omega}_{\mathbf{\omega}}^{-1/2}$ . As displayed in Fig. S9d and Table S3, the $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ of $\mathtt{C u O}$ precursor, $\mathsf{C u O@C u}$ -BTC (5, 10, 15, 20, and $25\mathrm{h}$ and pristine Cu-BTC after cycles were $2.89\times10^{-13}$ , $2.97\times10^{-13}$ , $5.08\times10^{-13}$ , $5.82\times10^{-13}$ , $1.98\times10^{-12}$ , $2.35\times10^{-13}$ , and $3.71\times10^{-13}\mathrm{cm}^{2}s^{-1}$ , respectively. The $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ increased to a maximum at $1.98\ \times\ 10^{-12}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ and decreased when the reaction continued for $25\mathrm{~h~}$ . The higher $\mathsf{D}_{\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ indicated better ion mobility, which was caused by the hierarchical structure, led to superior rate performance and cycle stability.[54]
CV measurements of CuO@Cu-BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ at various scan rates from 0.2 to $1.0\:\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1}$ were carried out to study further the kinetics of hierarchical multi-yolk-shell ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC (Fig. 4a). As expected, the peak current increased with a faster scanning rate. The capacitive contribution to the lithium storage can be quantitatively determined using the power-law relationship between peak current (i) and the scan rate (m), according to the following equations $\dot{\textbf{i}}=\mathsf{a v}^{\mathrm{b}}$ , where the b value of 0.5 indicates a diffusioncontrolled process and the b value of 1 suggests a surfacecontrolled or diffusion-irrelevant capacitive behavior.[55] As shown in Fig. 4b, the b value of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\ \mathrm{h})$ (0.82, 0.65 at cathodic scanning, and 0.78 at anodic peak) suggested favored pseudocapacitive kinetics.[56] In Fig. 4c, the pseudocapacitive contribution at the scan rate of $0.6\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ was $63.87\%$ . As the scan rate increased, the pseudocapacitive contribution grew bigger as expected, and finally reached a maximum value of $69.33\%$ at $1.0\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ (Fig. 4d). This higher capacitive contribution at high scan rates was attributed to nanosize $\mathtt{C u O}$ yolks, large specific surface area, and high porosity.[57,58] The pseudocapacitive capacitive process of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\mathrm{~h})$ enhanced the cycling stability and charge storage kinetics.[54]
The abovementioned performances of ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC $(20\mathrm{~h})$ based half cells were outstanding and stable. To test the potential of the hierarchical multi-yolk-shell CuO@Cu-BTC for practical application, full batteries based on the $\mathtt{C u O@C u}$ -BTC $(20\mathrm{~h})$ anode and the commercial $\mathsf{L i F e P O}_{4}$ cathode were assembled. Fig. 5a&b display the cycle performance and charge–discharge curves of the full battery at a rate of $0.2\mathrm{~C~}$ ( $1{\mathsf{C}}=170{\mathsf{m A}}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}$ ) between 0.3 and $4.0\mathrm{V}$ . The full battery delivered an initial discharge and charge specific capacities of 149.4 and $163.4\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ , respectively, corresponding to an initial coulombic efficiency of $91.4\%$ . The full battery showed slightly decreased specific capacity from 149.4 to $125.8\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 70cycles based on the mass of LiFe $\mathsf{P O}_{4}$ at 0.2 C, corresponding to a specific capacity retention of $84.2\%$ . The rate performance of the full battery based on $\mathsf{C u O@C u}$ -BTC (20 h) anode and ${\mathrm{LiFePO}}_{4}$ cathode is depicted in Fig. 5c&d. The specific capacities of the full battery were 156.5, 152.1, 142.7, 130.5, and $112.6\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~C},0.2\mathrm{~C},0.5\mathrm{~C},1.0\mathrm{~C},$ and $2.0~{\mathsf{C}},$ respectively, and recovered to $154\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ , when the rate went back to 0.1C. These results confirmed that the full batteries can retain the high specific capacity for numerous cycles, and indicated the chemical robustness of the electrodes. Considering their facile synthesis, well-defined structure, and high cyclability performance, the hierarchical ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC composites are promising for practical application in lithium-ion batteries.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, a simple way to obtain hierarchical ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC composites was developed to surmount the sluggish diffusion kinetics and the volume variation CuO and $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC. By adjusting the reaction time from 5 to $^{25\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the hierarchical structure and porosity of the obtained ${\mathsf{C u O@C u}}$ -BTC can be regulated to boost the specific capacity and cycle stability. The optimized CuO@CuBTC $(20\ \mathrm{~h~})$ delivered an ultrastable long-term performance of 780.7 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $(200\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ after 100 cycles, and 569 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}1000\ m A\ g^{-1}}.$ after 900 cycles, ranks among the best of TMOs and MOFs. The feasible in-situ synthesis can serve as a prospective strategy to fabricate other hierarchical composites as stable anodes for prospective application in LIBs.
